{
  "result": "There is a drawer in my kitchen that has no purpose. It's not the junk drawer \u2014 I have one of those, full of dead batteries and soy sauce packets. This one is empty. I keep it empty on purpose.\n\nI discovered it by accident when I moved in. Every other drawer slowly filled itself. Spoons. Towels. That weird avocado slicer I never use. But this one, the narrow one next to the stove, I decided to defend. No purpose. Just space.\n\nAnd it turns out an empty drawer is strangely powerful.\n\nWhen my brain feels too crowded, I open it. I look at the clean wood bottom and think: you can still choose to not fill something. In a world that is constantly trying to sell you organizers for your organizers, where your phone fills every spare second with infinite scroll, where every hobby has to become a side hustle, having one deliberately empty container feels like rebellion.\n\nMy friends think it's weird. One tried to put her keys in it. I said no. It's not anti-keys. It's pro-nothing.\n\nI think we all need an empty drawer. Not a minimalist aesthetic, not a decluttered lifestyle brand. Just a small, private, protected nothing. A place that proves you still know how to leave something alone. Where possibility can sit for a while without being forced to become productive.\n\nIt's the most useful useless thing I own. If you visit, I'll show you, but you can't put anything inside.",
